DATA['LA03']='''
procedure~Read: '1. Fold the paper. 2. Press the crease. 3. Open the paper.' Why are these instructions numbered?~To show the order of the actions.~To show which action is funniest.~To name the people in a story.~Numbering guides the reader through a procedure in sequence.
report~A page about frogs has sections called 'Body', 'Food' and 'Habitat'. How is it organised?~Facts are grouped by topic.~Events are told in the order they happened.~A problem is followed by a story ending.~Each heading gathers information about one aspect of frogs.
narrative~Read: 'One morning, a rabbit found a locked box. He searched for a key.' What makes this a story opening?~It introduces a character and a problem.~It lists materials for making a box.~It gives facts under science headings.~The rabbit and locked box set up characters and events for a narrative.
persuasion~Read: 'Our playground needs more shade. Trees would keep us cool. Please help us plant some.' How does the text support its request?~It gives a reason before asking people to help.~It lists the parts of a tree.~It tells a pretend adventure.~The reason explains why the writer wants the reader to act.
recount~Read: 'On Monday we visited the farm. Next we fed the hens. Finally we went home.' What helps organise this recount?~Time words put past events in order.~Headings sort animals by size.~Rhyming words tell us what to sing.~Next and finally signal the sequence of events that already happened.
recipe_features~Which opening best suits a recipe?~You will need: oats, milk and a bowl.~Once upon a time, a bowl ran away.~I believe bowls should be blue.~A recipe commonly starts with what is needed before the method.
comparison~Read: 'Both buses and trains carry passengers. Trains use tracks, but buses use roads.' Which words signal a difference?~but buses use roads~Both buses and trains~carry passengers~But introduces the contrasting feature of buses.
shared_feature~Read: 'A snail has a shell. A slug does not. Both have soft bodies.' Which information belongs under 'Both'?~soft bodies~a shell~no shell~Both explicitly marks the shared feature.
purpose_language~Which sentence belongs in directions for planting a seed?~Make a small hole in the soil.~The seed dreamed of being a giant.~I think this is the prettiest seed.~The action verb make tells the reader what to do.
report_language~Which sentence suits a factual report about penguins?~Penguins have feathers and wings.~Please buy my penguin badge today!~The penguin invited the moon to tea.~The sentence supplies factual information rather than persuasion or fantasy.
headings~A text explains how to care for a pet fish. Where should 'Give a small amount of food each day' go?~Feeding~Tank cleaning~Buying equipment~The information concerns food, so the feeding heading groups it logically.
same_topic~Text A gives numbered steps for making a kite. Text B tells how a kite escaped from a child. How do their structures differ?~A orders instructions; B follows story events.~A tells a story; B lists instructions.~Both must be shopping lists.~The purpose of making something differs from narrating an event.
invitation~An invitation says 'Art show, Friday, 3 pm, school hall'. Why are these details grouped together?~They tell guests what, when and where.~They explain how paint is made.~They describe the ending of a story.~An invitation makes event information easy for guests to find.
poem_lines~A poem places short lines under one another and repeats a line after each verse. What can this arrangement help a reader notice?~The pattern and repeated rhythm.~The price of each word.~The dates when events happened.~Line breaks and repeated lines can shape the pattern of a poem.
science_sequence~A science text has headings 'Question', 'What we did' and 'What we noticed'. What is its main organising job?~To separate the investigation's parts.~To rank the funniest characters.~To list books alphabetically.~The headings distinguish the enquiry, method and observations.
maths_explanation~Read: 'I made ten first. Then I added the remaining two. The total was twelve.' Why does this maths explanation use 'first' and 'then'?~To show the order of the thinking.~To describe where the numbers live.~To persuade someone to buy counters.~Sequence words help the reader follow the method.
persuasive_reason~A poster says, 'Walk to school when you can.' Which addition gives a reason supporting its purpose?~Walking gives us exercise.~Our school has a long name.~Yesterday I found a pebble.~The reason explains a benefit of the requested action.
story_resolution~A story begins with a lost puppy. Which event would resolve that problem?~The puppy's owner finds it and takes it home.~A second puppy appears on another street.~The weather becomes cloudy.~A resolution addresses the central problem introduced in the story.
instructions_repair~Instructions say, 'Put the lid on. Fill the bottle.' What change helps their purpose?~Put filling before closing the lid.~Replace every verb with a colour.~Remove both actions.~The order must let the reader complete the task successfully.
letter_organisation~A letter begins 'Dear Uncle' and ends 'Love from Kim'. What do these parts do?~Identify who is addressed and who is writing.~List materials and tools.~Show two chapter titles.~A greeting and closing frame a personal message between people.
compare_formats~A timetable puts activities beside times. A recount describes a finished excursion in sentences. Why are they arranged differently?~One helps plan when; the other tells what happened.~Every text must use a different font.~Timetables cannot contain words.~Organisation follows what the reader needs the text to do.
choose_structure~You want classmates to learn how to play a new game. Which plan best suits that purpose?~Equipment, aim, then ordered rules.~Characters, a mystery, then a surprise ending.~Opinion, unrelated joke, then a list of colours.~A game explanation needs materials, purpose and steps or rules.
@structure_apply~Choose a simple classroom task. Tell an adult how you would organise written instructions for it. Give a heading and at least two steps.~Example: 'Packing my bag': check the timetable, then put in the books needed.~Accept a relevant heading and sensible ordered actions; the child should explain that the sequence helps someone do the task.
@compare_apply~Compare a storybook with a recipe you have, or use these descriptions: a story follows a lost cat; a recipe lists ingredients and steps. Explain two differences in organisation or language.~Example: a story introduces a character and follows events; a recipe lists ingredients and uses action instructions.~Accept two accurate differences linked to the texts' purposes, not only cover colour or length.
test_procedure~'You need a cup and water. Pour the water into the cup.' What feature shows that this text guides an action?~It names materials and tells the reader what to do.~It introduces a hero and a villain.~It gives an opinion followed by a vote.~Materials and imperative action language support a procedure.
test_information~A butterfly report uses 'Life cycle' and 'Food' as headings. Why?~To group related facts so readers can follow each topic.~To tell readers which butterfly is the best.~To give every sentence a character's name.~Headings organise informative content into related sections.
test_narrative~Read: 'At sunset, a tiny dragon could not find its flame.' Which feature sets up a story?~A character faces a difficulty.~A timetable lists the sunset time.~A recipe lists its ingredients.~A character and difficulty can drive narrative events.
test_persuasion~Which order best suits a short letter asking for a bike rack?~Request, reasons it would help, closing.~Ingredients, mixing steps, cooking time.~Setting, magical event, fairy-tale ending.~A persuasive request is supported by reasons directed to the reader.
test_recount~A child writes about yesterday's swim. Which opening makes the recount clear?~Yesterday our class walked to the pool.~Tomorrow we might swim.~Please build a new swimming pool.~Yesterday introduces a completed event, fitting a recount.
test_comparison~Read: 'Paper bags and cloth bags can carry shopping. Unlike paper bags, cloth bags can be washed.' How is the information organised?~A shared use is followed by a difference.~An instruction is followed by a recipe.~A greeting is followed by a farewell.~The text compares a common purpose and contrasts washability.
test_signal~Which phrase would help introduce a contrast in a text about two bikes?~The smaller bike, however, has no gears.~Both bikes have wheels.~The bikes are alike in colour.~However signals that the next information differs.
test_subject~A history recount lists 'First', 'Later' and 'Years afterwards'. What do these words help show?~The sequence of events over time.~The alphabetical order of people's names.~The equipment needed for an experiment.~Time signals help readers follow historical events in order.
test_poem~A chant repeats 'Pass it on!' after each short verse. How does the repeated line help its purpose?~It gives listeners a part they can join in with.~It tells readers where to find an index.~It separates ingredients from a method.~A repeated refrain supports shared chanting and a predictable pattern.
test_heading~A report section explains how a koala rests during the day. Which heading fits?~Resting~Finding food~Growing young~A useful heading names the section's actual topic.
test_change_purpose~You turn facts about recycling into a poster encouraging recycling. What language would support the new purpose?~A clear request such as 'Put clean paper in the recycling bin.'~Only the names of story characters.~A list of unrelated past events.~A persuasive poster asks the audience to act.
test_order~A method ends with 'Check that the glue is dry.' Why should this come after joining the pieces?~The glue needs to have been used before its drying can be checked.~The final line must always contain five words.~Checking glue belongs only in a story.~The sequence follows the practical order of the task.
test_explanation~A maths page shows a worked sum followed by 'I grouped the tens first.' What does the sentence add?~An explanation of the method.~An invitation to an event.~A character's imaginary problem.~The language explains how the answer was reached.
test_pair~A weather report gives today's conditions. A poem describes rain tapping like fingers. Why does their language differ?~The report informs; the poem creates an image or sound experience.~The report cannot mention rain.~The poem must give exact temperatures.~Different purposes lead to factual reporting or imaginative description.
@test_structure~Describe a plan for a short report about a familiar animal. Give two section headings and explain what belongs under each.~Example: 'Food' for what it eats and 'Home' for where it lives.~Accept two distinct, relevant categories with matching information and an explanation that headings group facts.
@test_compare~Explain how a class excursion invitation would be organised differently from a recount written after the excursion.~Example: the invitation gives event, time and place; the recount tells past events in order.~Accept a purpose-linked comparison including useful invitation details and sequencing of completed events.
'''
DATA['LA04']='''
personal_subject~Read: 'The chicks hatched today. They are fluffy.' What does 'They' refer to?~the chicks~today~the nest~They carries the reference to the plural noun chicks into the next sentence.
personal_object~Read: 'I found a shell and washed it.' What was washed?~the shell~the speaker~the beach~It replaces shell, keeping the two actions connected.
speaker_group~A speaker and two friends built a cubby. Complete: '___ built it together.'~We~She~It~We includes the speaker and other people.
object_group~Read: 'The books were muddy. Dad cleaned them.' Which word avoids repeating 'the books'?~them~Dad~cleaned~Them refers back to the books as the things being cleaned.
pronoun_replace~Read: 'The puppy barked. The puppy wanted dinner.' Which second sentence avoids repeating the noun?~It wanted dinner.~Dinner wanted the puppy.~They wanted the puppy.~It keeps the puppy as the subject without repeating its name.
possessive_mine~Mina says, 'This scarf belongs to me. It is ___.'~mine~yours~theirs~Mine expresses possession by the speaker.
possessive_ours~You and your brother share a tent. You say, 'That tent is ___.'~ours~hers~his~Ours refers to something belonging to the speaker and another person or people.
possessive_theirs~Read: 'The players own these caps. The caps are theirs.' Who owns the caps?~the players~one unnamed visitor~the caps~Theirs refers back to the plural owners, the players.
possessive_reference~Read: 'Nora packed her boots.' Whose boots does 'her' refer to here?~Nora's~the reader's~the teacher's~With Nora as the only person named, her links the boots to Nora.
possessive_contrast~Kai owns the red bike. Ella owns the blue one. Ella says, 'The blue bike is mine; the red bike is ___.'~his~hers~ours~His refers to Kai's ownership; mine refers to Ella as the speaker.
ellipsis_object~Read: 'Would you like a pear?' 'Yes, please.' What does the short reply mean?~Yes, I would like a pear, please.~Yes, I have planted a tree.~Yes, the pear is blue.~The question supplies the words omitted from the natural short answer.
ellipsis_verb~Read: 'I can swim, and my cousin can too.' What can the cousin do?~swim~read~run~Can too leaves out swim because the first clause already supplies it.
ellipsis_noun~Read: 'I chose the green cup. Tessa chose the yellow.' What word is understood after 'yellow'?~cup~coat~flower~Cup is carried over from the earlier sentence, so it need not be repeated.
ellipsis_subject~Read: 'The dog stretched and yawned.' Who yawned?~the dog~an unnamed child~the reader~The dog is the understood subject of both linked actions.
short_answer~Read: 'Who has the tickets?' 'Uncle Pat.' Which full answer has the same meaning?~Uncle Pat has the tickets.~Uncle Pat wants some tickets.~The tickets have Uncle Pat.~The short reply omits has the tickets because it is clear from the question.
avoid_ambiguity~Read: 'Lena met Rose. She carried a red bag.' You mean Rose carried it. Which change makes this certain?~Lena met Rose. Rose carried a red bag.~Lena met Rose. She carried it.~Lena met Rose. Someone carried something.~Repeating Rose removes ambiguity when she could refer to either person.
clear_reference~Which pair makes 'it' easy to understand?~A balloon floated up. It caught on a branch.~A balloon passed a kite. It was red.~A balloon passed a ball. It was round.~The first pair has one clear object for it to refer back to.
pronoun_agreement~Read: 'The children put on raincoats. ___ walked outside.'~They~It~He~They agrees with the plural children and keeps the reference clear.
cohesion_edit~Which version keeps the meaning with less repetition?~Mum found the keys and put them in her bag.~Mum found the keys and put Mum in the keys.~Mum found them and put it in them.~Them clearly replaces keys; her refers to Mum.
omission_limit~Read: 'I saw a bird beside a plane. It was blue.' Why might a reader be unsure?~It could refer to the bird or the plane.~Blue cannot describe anything.~Every sentence needs a person's name.~Two possible nouns make the reference ambiguous without further context.
restore_words~Read: 'Zoe chose two apples, and I chose three.' What did the speaker choose three of?~apples~bowls~oranges~Apples is omitted after three because the earlier clause supplies that meaning.
ellipsis_meaning~Read: 'Will you come on Friday?' 'I hope so.' What does 'so' stand for?~that I will come on Friday~that Friday has been cancelled~that I came yesterday~So refers to the idea in the question without repeating the whole clause.
@rewrite_cohesion~Rewrite or say this with less repetition: 'The kittens found a box. The kittens climbed into the box.' Keep it clear who did what.~The kittens found a box. They climbed into it.~Accept a clear rewrite using they for kittens and it for box, or another unambiguous cohesive wording that preserves both actions.
@restore_ellipsis~Read: 'Have you finished your puzzle?' 'Not yet.' Say the full meaning of 'Not yet' and explain why the shorter reply is understandable.~I have not finished my puzzle yet. The question tells us which action and object the reply means.~Accept the restored meaning and an explanation referring to the context provided by the question.
test_reference~Read: 'A crab hid beneath a rock. It came out at dusk.' What came out?~the crab~dusk~the shore~The action and the earlier subject connect it to the crab.
test_object~Read: 'The parcels arrived. We carried them inside.' What does 'them' replace?~the parcels~we~inside~Them keeps the reference to the parcels as the things being carried.
test_speaker~A child says, 'This drawing belongs to my sister and me. It is ___.'~ours~yours~theirs~Ours includes the speaker among the owners.
test_possession~Read: 'I brought my umbrella. You brought yours.' What does 'yours' mean?~your umbrella~my umbrella~their coats~Yours replaces the possessive phrase your umbrella.
test_owner~Read: 'The neighbours parked their caravan.' Whose caravan is meant?~the neighbours'~the writer's~the caravan's~Their links the caravan to the plural owners already named.
test_rewrite~Choose the clearest rewrite: 'The dancer bowed. The dancer thanked the crowd.'~The dancer bowed and thanked the crowd.~The crowd bowed and thanked the dancer.~The dancer thanked it and bowed them.~The shared subject can be understood for both actions without changing who acts.
test_ellipsis~Read: 'Sam ordered soup; Lee ordered salad.' Which shorter version preserves the meaning?~Sam ordered soup; Lee, salad.~Sam ordered Lee; soup, salad.~Sam soup; ordered Lee.~Ordered can be inferred in the second part while the two orders remain clear.
test_short_reply~Read: 'Where is the torch?' 'In the drawer.' What words are understood before the reply?~The torch is~The drawer wants~Where did~The short answer omits the torch is because the question supplies the topic.
test_can~Read: 'Asha cannot whistle, but I can.' What can the speaker do?~whistle~hear Asha~draw a picture~Can refers back to whistle, the action already stated.
test_ambiguity~'I put the mug beside the jug. It was cracked.' You mean the jug. Which revision is clear?~I put the mug beside the cracked jug.~I put it beside it. It was cracked.~I put the mug beside the jug. It was still cracked.~Naming the jug as cracked removes the competing reference.
test_plural~Read: 'The ducks reached the pond. ___ slipped into the water.'~They~He~It~They refers back to the plural ducks.
test_link~Read: 'Our class made a banner. The banner is ours.' How does 'ours' link the sentences?~It refers to the class as the owners.~It gives a new name to the banner.~It says the banner belongs to another school.~Ours links possession to the speaker's class already mentioned.
test_omitted_noun~Read: 'I wore the woollen hat, not the cotton.' What is understood after 'cotton'?~hat~coat~sock~The earlier noun hat supplies the omitted noun in the contrast.
test_safe_omission~Which short reply clearly answers 'Would you like some water?'~Yes, please.~Under the table.~Next to the library.~The question makes the omitted request for water clear in Yes, please.
@test_rewrite~Rewrite: 'The robot picked up the tools. The robot put the tools on the shelf.' Use pronouns where the meaning stays clear.~The robot picked up the tools. It put them on the shelf.~Accept a clear version using it for robot and them for tools, with both original actions preserved.
@test_expand~Read: 'Who will carry the picnic basket?' 'I will.' Tell an adult what the reply means in full and how you know.~I will carry the picnic basket. The question supplies the action left out of the reply.~Accept a complete meaning and a reference to the question as the source of the omitted words.
'''
DATA['LA05']='''
contents_page~Contents: 'Getting started — 3; Feeding a rabbit — 8; Keeping its home clean — 14.' Where would you begin reading about food?~page 8~page 3~page 14~The table of contents links the feeding chapter to page 8.
chapter_choice~Chapters: '1 A Strange Noise; 2 The Search; 3 Home at Last.' Which chapter would you revisit for the search?~Chapter 2~Chapter 1~Chapter 3~The chapter title identifies the part about searching.
index_specific~Index: 'beaks 6, 12; feathers 4; nests 9.' Which pages might tell you about beaks?~6 and 12~4 and 9~only 9~An index can list more than one page for the same topic.
index_letters~An index is alphabetical. Where should 'dolphins' appear?~Between 'crabs' and 'eels'.~Before 'ants'.~After 'whales'.~The starting letters c, d and e show the correct alphabetical position.
contents_vs_index~You want a chapter overview of a whole book before reading it. Which feature helps most?~Table of contents~Index entry for one word~Page number on the final page~A table of contents lists the main sections in their reading order.
index_vs_contents~You want every page that mentions 'nectar', even inside different chapters. Which feature is designed for that?~Index~Front-cover picture~Chapter number alone~An index points from a specific topic to the pages where it appears.
sidebar~A website side-bar lists 'Animals', 'Plants' and 'Weather'. Where should you look for an article about rain?~Weather~Plants~Animals~The menu labels group topics, and rain belongs under weather.
dropdown~A button says 'Choose a year'. Opening it shows 'Year 1', 'Year 2', 'Year 3'. What should a Year 2 reader select?~Year 2~Year 1~Year 3~The drop-down options let the reader select the required year level.
link_label~A page has links 'Listen to the story', 'Meet the author' and 'Print the map'. Which opens information about the writer?~Meet the author~Listen to the story~Print the map~Author means writer, so the label predicts the destination.
menu_path~Home menu: 'Learn' and 'Shop'. Under Learn: 'Birds' and 'Rocks'. Which path leads to bird facts?~Learn, then Birds~Shop, then checkout~Learn, then Rocks~The reader first selects the learning section, then the specific topic.
page_range~Contents: 'Tide pools — 5; Sand dunes — 11; Cliffs — 18.' Which chapter includes page 14?~Sand dunes~Tide pools~Cliffs~Sand dunes starts at 11 and the next chapter starts at 18, so 14 is within it.
index_range~Index: 'seeds 7–9'. What does this entry tell you to check?~Pages 7, 8 and 9.~Only pages 7 and 9.~Chapters 7 and 9 only.~A page range includes the pages between its endpoints.
chapter_heading~You open a book at page 20 and see 'Chapter 4: Winter'. How can that heading help?~It confirms which chapter you are reading.~It gives the page of every word in the book.~It tells you the book's price.~A chapter heading identifies the current section of the text.
link_purpose~The words 'See the full timetable' are a link beneath a short event notice. What would you expect after selecting it?~A more complete schedule.~A list of the author's pets.~A blank drawing page.~The link label describes the information at its destination.
sidebar_return~You are reading a page about frogs. The side-bar still shows 'Home', 'Animals' and 'Plants'. To browse other animals, which label would you choose?~Animals~Plants~Home only because it is first~The Animals section is the most direct menu route to related animal pages.
dropdown_category~A museum page has a 'Visit' drop-down with 'Opening hours', 'Tickets' and 'Getting here'. Which option helps plan a bus trip to it?~Getting here~Tickets~Opening hours~Getting here is the option about reaching the location.
index_lookup~Index: 'bats 10; bees 5; beetles 6; birds 12'. Which entry would you use for beetles?~beetles 6~bees 5~birds 12~Match the whole word, not just its first letters, before using the page number.
contents_order~Contents lists 'Planning — 2; Building — 6; Testing — 10'. What does this order help a reader follow?~The main sections from planning to testing.~An alphabetical list of every word.~The authors from youngest to oldest.~Contents follows the book's section order, which here follows a project sequence.
multiple_routes~You need the 'Habitat' chapter. The contents gives page 16, and a screen version has a 'Habitat' link. How can you reach it in each version?~Turn to page 16 in print; select the link on screen.~Shake the book; change the screen brightness.~Use page 16 as a password on both versions.~Print page references and screen links are different navigation tools for the same section.
wrong_destination~You select 'Games' but need a fact page. The side-bar offers 'Facts'. What is the most direct next step?~Select Facts.~Keep choosing random games.~Close the book on your desk.~A relevant navigation label helps correct the route efficiently.
contents_selection~Contents: 'Before the walk — 4; Along the track — 9; After the walk — 17'. Where should you look for preparation advice?~Before the walk~After the walk~Along the track only~The heading before the walk signals information needed in preparation.
link_vs_heading~A screen heading says 'Whales'. Below it, a link says 'Read about whale sounds'. Which part would you select to reach the sound article?~Read about whale sounds~The page margin~The browser clock~The labelled link is the navigation control described in the question.
@navigate_print~With an adult, use a book with a contents page. Choose a chapter, say its starting page and turn to it. Show how you know you reached the right chapter.~The child matches a contents entry to its page and checks the chapter heading.~Accept successful navigation using an actual book. If none is available, the adult may make three numbered paper sections with a contents list; do not award completion for naming a feature only.
@navigate_screen~With an adult, use a suitable website or a paper mock-up of a website with a side-bar and a drop-down. Choose a topic and show the route using both controls.~The child selects a relevant side-bar category, opens the drop-down and chooses the matching option.~Accept a demonstrated route with explanation of the selected labels. A prepared paper interface is valid if the child actually follows its labelled choices.
test_contents~Contents: 'The coast — 4; The desert — 13; The forest — 21'. Where should you turn to begin the desert chapter?~page 13~page 4~page 21~The desert entry points to its start on page 13.
test_index~Index: 'comets 8, 19; moons 11; stars 3–6'. Where should you check for comets?~pages 8 and 19~pages 3 to 6~page 11 only~The numbers beside comets identify both listed locations.
test_chapter~A story has 'Chapter 5: The Rescue' and 'Chapter 6: The Journey Home'. Which chapter would you choose to reread how someone was saved?~Chapter 5~Chapter 6~Neither, because titles cannot help~The rescue title points to the saving event.
test_sidebar~A school site side-bar lists 'News', 'Calendar' and 'Class work'. Which is the best first choice for dates of upcoming events?~Calendar~Class work~News only because it comes first~A calendar is organised around dates and scheduled events.
test_dropdown~A library's 'Borrow' menu opens 'Books', 'Audiobooks' and 'Magazines'. Which option helps you find a story to listen to?~Audiobooks~Magazines~Books without checking their format~Audiobooks provide recorded reading, matching the listening goal.
test_link~You want to hear how a new word sounds. Which link label best matches?~Listen to the word~Read our privacy notice~View the school map~The label explicitly offers the pronunciation or spoken word.
test_feature_choice~You remember a small mention of 'camouflage' but not its chapter. What should you check first if the book has one?~The index entry for camouflage.~The illustrator's name.~The back cover colour.~An index can locate a specific topic without knowing its chapter.
test_overview~A reader wants to see which major topics a book covers. What should they scan?~The table of contents.~A single footnote.~Only the last sentence.~The contents offers an overview of the book's sections.
test_range~Contents: 'Tracks — 6; Nests — 12; Burrows — 20'. Page 17 is most likely in which section?~Nests~Tracks~Burrows~The Nests section starts at 12 and continues until the next section at 20.
test_alphabetical~Where would 'lizards' go in an alphabetical index?~After 'leaves' and before 'logs'.~After 'zebras'.~Before 'ants'.~Within the l entries, le comes before li, and li before lo.
test_nested_menu~On a nature site choose 'Explore', then a menu offers 'Ocean' and 'Bushland'. Where would you go for rock-pool animals?~Explore, then Ocean.~Explore, then Bushland.~Choose the shopping basket.~Rock pools are coastal, so the Ocean category is the relevant route.
test_verify~You follow a contents entry to page 25. What should you check to confirm the destination?~The chapter heading or section title.~Whether the page feels thick.~Whether the page number is your favourite.~The heading confirms the page belongs to the intended section.
test_link_prediction~A link says 'Download the walking map'. What would you expect it to provide?~A map file for the walk.~A new chapter about cooking.~A change to your device's volume.~The link describes both an action, download, and the content, the map.
test_topic_match~A drop-down under 'Care for your pet' shows 'Food', 'Exercise' and 'Health checks'. Which should you select for advice about daily walks?~Exercise~Food~Health checks as the only possible route~Walking is an exercise topic, making it the most direct choice.
@test_print_route~Use a book's index to find a topic and turn to one listed page. Show the adult the information you found and explain how the entry helped.~The child uses a topic entry and its page number, then locates relevant information on that page.~Accept a real book or an adult-prepared numbered paper text with an index. Require demonstrated use, not a memorised definition.
@test_screen_route~On an adult-approved website or prepared paper screen, find a menu link leading to a chosen topic. Follow it and explain how you checked the destination.~The child predicts the destination from the label, follows the link and checks the resulting title or content.~Accept successful navigation and checking. Do not require typing credentials, downloading files or visiting unfamiliar sites.
'''
DATA['LA01']='''
request_help~You missed the last instruction because a truck passed the window. What could you say to the teacher?~Could you repeat the last step, please?~I have finished every step.~You never explain anything.~A specific request tells the teacher which help you need without blaming anyone.
friend_request~Your friend is finishing a drawing with the ruler you need. What could you ask?~May I have a turn when you finish?~Give it here right now.~Why are you drawing that?~A friendly request respects the friend's current turn and asks for the next one.
host_role~You are welcoming families to a class display. Which opening suits your role?~Welcome. Let me show you our work.~Everyone must copy my picture.~I need someone to mark my spelling.~A host welcomes visitors and offers to guide them.
leader_role~You are leading a group making a tower. Which words help the group begin?~Let's put the wide blocks at the bottom.~You can all watch me do it.~My tower will be better than yours.~A leader can suggest a clear shared action and include the group.
listener_role~A visitor is explaining how she trains rescue dogs. You want to ask a question. What could you say when she pauses?~May I ask how a dog learns to find someone?~Stop! My pet is much cuter.~I already know about all dogs.~A relevant question at a pause shows interest and respects the speaker's turn.
disagreement~A classmate thinks the story ends sadly. You think it ends happily. Which reply keeps the discussion respectful?~I thought it was happy because the friends met again.~You did not understand the story at all.~You must change your answer to mine.~Explaining your view with a story detail allows disagreement without insulting the other reader.
apology~You spoke over someone during show-and-tell. Which response repairs the interruption?~Sorry I interrupted. Please finish what you were saying.~I was only talking a little.~Next time, speak faster.~The apology names the action and gives the speaker their turn back.
unfamiliar_listener~You call the school office about a lost lunchbox. Which opening helps someone who does not know you?~Hello, I am Ari from 2C. I lost a red lunchbox.~It is me. Have you found it?~You know that thing I lost?~Giving your name, class and the item helps an unfamiliar listener understand.
younger_listener~A younger buddy does not know what 'return' means. Which instruction helps?~Please put the book back on this shelf.~Please return the item to its usual location.~Please do the usual thing.~Familiar words and a clear place make the direction easier for a younger listener.
urgent_context~A ball rolls towards the road and a friend starts to chase it. Which words fit the urgent moment?~Stop! Stay on the footpath!~When you have time, could we have a chat?~I wonder whose ball that might be.~An urgent warning needs immediate, clear words. Respectful language can be firm.
quiet_context~You need a tissue while everyone is reading quietly. Which approach fits the room?~Quietly ask the person beside you for a tissue.~Shout your request across the room.~Announce your request to every table.~The request stays clear while its volume suits the quiet setting.
role_change~At home, Max says, 'Hi, Gran!' On stage, Max says, 'Good evening, everyone.' What changed?~Max is greeting a familiar person first and an audience next.~Max has forgotten how to greet Gran.~Greetings must always use the longest words.~The audience and Max's role explain the different greetings.
polite_refusal~Someone asks to borrow your special pen, but you need it now. Which reply is clear and kind?~I need this one now, but you may borrow my spare.~Take it, although I cannot finish without it.~You should never ask me for anything.~A respectful refusal can state a boundary and offer another option.
feedback_role~A partner asks how to make a sign easier to read. Which comment helps?~The letters could be larger so people can read them from the door.~Your sign is terrible.~My sign has more colours.~Useful feedback names a change and explains how it would help.
clarify_meaning~Your coach says, 'Move back a little.' You are unsure where to stand. What could you ask?~Should I stand behind this line?~Why do we have a coach?~Can we change the team name?~The question checks the unclear part of the instruction.
invitation_context~You are inviting the principal to a class performance. Which message gives a respectful invitation?~Our class would like to invite you to our play on Friday.~You have to come because I said so.~Come over sometime, mate.~A school invitation suits the listener and gives a useful event detail.
speaker_purpose~A shop assistant asks, 'How can I help?' Which reply fits your role as a customer looking for glue?~Could you show me where the glue is, please?~Please take your seats and listen.~Welcome to my classroom.~A customer can explain what they need and ask for help.
repair_words~You say, 'Move!' and your partner looks upset. Which new wording keeps your request but softens it?~Could you move your chair so I can get past, please?~Move, move, move!~Why are you always in my way?~A clear request with a reason is more considerate than a bare command.
include_others~As discussion leader, you notice one child has not spoken. What could you say?~Would you like to share an idea, or shall we come back to you?~You must speak right now.~We only need ideas from fast speakers.~The invitation offers a turn without forcing an immediate response.
tone_context~'Please sit here' can be a welcome or an instruction. What would help you tell which it is?~Who is speaking, their tone and what is happening.~Only the number of letters in 'please'.~Only whether the chair is blue.~Context and tone help listeners understand the purpose of the same words.
audience_detail~Your sister knows where 'our usual spot' is. A new pupil does not. What should you tell the new pupil?~Meet me beside the library door.~Meet me at our usual spot.~Meet me where you know I mean.~A new listener needs an explicit location rather than shared family knowledge.
role_pair~Which pair fits a child asking for help and then acting as a helper?~'Can you show me?' / 'Let's try the first step together.'~'Watch me do it.' / 'I need someone to show me.'~'Go away.' / 'That is your problem.'~The first utterance asks for support; the second offers support in the new role.
@spoken_request~Say two requests for help opening a box: one to a close friend and one to a visiting adult. Tell your adult helper what you changed.~Example: 'Can you help me with this?' and 'Excuse me, could you help me open this box, please?' Explain that the visitor may need more context.~Accept two understandable, respectful requests and a sensible explanation about listener familiarity, wording or tone. Do not require formal wording in every adult interaction.
@role_play~With an adult, pretend you are first a team leader and then a team member. Suggest how to share the building blocks in each role.~Example leader: 'Let's give each pair four blocks.' Example member: 'Could our pair have four blocks too?'~Accept role-appropriate contributions that address sharing. The child should show how their words fit leading or participating, rather than merely repeat an identical line.
audience_test~You meet a new librarian while your friend waits nearby. Which words help introduce yourself?~Hello, I am Remy from Year 2. Could you help me find a book?~You remember what I like, don't you?~Hey, you! Pick something.~An introduction supplies information a new listener does not yet know.
repair_test~You gave a classmate the wrong meeting time. What could you say?~I am sorry. I told you two o'clock, but we meet at one.~You should have guessed the right time.~The time does not matter now.~The speaker takes responsibility and gives the corrected information.
purpose_test~A child tells a friend, 'Great save!' after a game. Which purpose best fits these words?~Praising the friend's action.~Requesting a new ball.~Giving directions to the court.~The brief friendly comment congratulates someone in the game context.
role_test~You are interviewing a gardener for a class report. Which question fits your role?~How do you decide when the seedlings need water?~Can you write my whole report for me?~Why is my friend sitting there?~An interviewer asks a relevant question that gathers information from the guest.
emergency_test~A child feels faint during sport. Which message helps the teacher act?~Please help. Someone feels faint beside the goal.~Something happened somewhere.~We are all doing sport now.~A helpful urgent message names the problem and the location.
disagree_test~Your group wants a long title. You think a short one is easier to read. What could you say?~Could we try a shorter title so it fits on one line?~Everyone else's titles are silly.~I will rub yours out without asking.~The suggestion explains a reason and invites the group to consider it.
boundary_test~A friend keeps tapping your arm while you work. Which words set a respectful boundary?~Please stop tapping my arm. I need to finish this.~You are not allowed to talk to anyone.~I will tap you back harder.~A firm request names the behaviour to stop without threatening the friend.
setting_test~A teacher asks the same class to cheer outdoors and speak softly in a museum. Why do the directions differ?~The settings call for different sound levels.~Children cannot use the same voices twice.~Museums do not allow any questions.~Language and voice are adjusted to the setting and the needs of others.
explain_test~A visitor asks where the toilets are. Which reply gives helpful directions?~Go along this hall, then turn left at the office.~Over there, where they always are.~Somewhere past the rooms.~Specific directions help someone unfamiliar with the building.
welcome_test~A child joins your game for the first time. Which comment helps them join in?~Welcome! We take turns throwing from this line.~You should already know our rules.~We started, so you cannot learn now.~A welcome and a short explanation support a newcomer.
thanks_test~A classmate stayed to help you tidy spilled paint. Which thanks names what you appreciate?~Thanks for helping me clean the paint off the table.~Thanks for everything that ever happened.~You finished after everyone else.~Specific thanks connects appreciation to the helpful action.
listen_test~During a partner's explanation you realise you misunderstood. Which reply checks meaning?~Do you mean we fold it before we cut it?~I will just do something else.~You are explaining it wrong.~Checking your understanding keeps the interaction focused and respectful.
written_context_test~A message goes to every family in the class. Which wording is clearest?~Please bring a hat for our walk on Tuesday.~Bring that thing tomorrow, like we said.~You all know what to bring.~A group message gives explicit details rather than assuming shared knowledge.
role_compare_test~A pupil says, 'May I have a turn?' The game organiser says, 'Your turn is next.' What do their words show?~One requests a turn; the other manages the turns.~Both are asking to leave the game.~Only one person is being respectful.~Different responsibilities lead to different but equally respectful language.
@spoken_test~Tell an adult how you would ask a busy teacher to explain a task, then how you would explain that task to a younger child.~Example: 'When you are ready, could you explain the first step?' Then use short, clear words to guide the younger child.~Accept a considerate help request and a helpful explanation suited to the younger listener. Ask the child to explain the difference in their roles.
@context_test~Role-play greeting a friend at the playground and welcoming a guest to a class exhibition. Explain one language choice you made.~Example: a relaxed familiar greeting to the friend and a welcome with an offer to show the guest around.~Accept two suitable greetings and an explanation linked to audience, relationship or hosting role. Accent and home-language differences are not errors.
'''
DATA['LA02']='''
preference_reason~Read: 'The beetle wore boots and danced on a spoon.' Which response explains enjoyment of the line?~I like the silly picture of a beetle dancing in boots.~The line is printed in a book.~I have seen a beetle before.~The response links enjoyment to a specific amusing image in the words.
sound_response~Read aloud: 'Tip, tap, tiny rain; tip, tap, on the pane.' Which reason supports liking its sound?~The repeated 'tip, tap' sounds like little raindrops.~The poem tells me where to buy a window.~The line has no repeated words.~The reader connects the repeated sound pattern with rain.
character_response~Read: 'The gate was heavy. Ada held it open until everyone had passed.' Which appreciation fits?~I like Ada's kindness because she waits for everyone.~I like Ada because she wins the race.~I like Ada's joke about the gate.~Holding the gate for others is evidence of consideration; no race or joke appears.
setting_response~Read: 'Soft moss covered the path. A stream whispered beside it.' Which response uses details to explain a feeling?~I enjoyed the peaceful setting because of the soft moss and quiet stream.~I enjoyed the crowded setting because everyone shouted.~I enjoyed the setting because it was a setting.~The response names language that creates a calm impression.
information_response~A page explains how a seed grows and labels its root and shoot. Which reason explains why a reader values the page?~The labels help me tell the root from the shoot.~The page must be good because it has a number.~Every page about seeds is my favourite.~The reader identifies a feature and explains how it helps understanding.
precise_appreciation~Which word best completes this response? 'The puppy kept slipping on the shiny floor. That scene made me laugh; it was ___. '~amusing~frightening~lonely~Amusing describes something that makes the reader laugh.
preference_comparison~Text A lists facts about clouds. Text B follows a cloud on an imaginary journey. Which response gives a reason for preferring B?~I prefer B because I enjoy imagining an adventure.~I prefer B because A is a text.~B is better and nobody may disagree.~The reader connects a personal preference with the imaginative feature of B.
different_preferences~Jay enjoys a fast chase in a story. Lee enjoys the quiet ending. Which response respects both views?~We enjoyed different parts, and we can explain why.~Only the first reader's opinion counts.~We must choose the same favourite part.~Text preferences can differ while still being supported with reasons.
dislike_reason~A story changes location so often that a reader loses track. Which response explains a dislike usefully?~I found the quick changes confusing because I could not tell where the characters were.~The author is a bad person.~Everyone must dislike this book.~The response explains the effect of a text feature without attacking the author.
evidence_choice~A reader says, 'I liked the surprising ending.' Which ending supports that reason?~The missing crown was on the queen's head all along.~The queen put on the crown exactly as planned.~The crown was described on the first page.~An unexpected discovery explains the reader's sense of surprise.
strength_of_feeling~Which response expresses stronger enjoyment than 'I liked the story'?~I loved the story and wanted to read it again.~I noticed that the story had pages.~I did not enjoy the story.~Loved and wanting to reread express stronger enthusiasm than liked.
gentle_vocabulary~Read: 'The moon rocked the sleepy sea.' Which response best explains the gentle language?~I found it soothing because it made me imagine slow, calm movement.~I found it noisy because it described a loud crash.~I found it useful because it listed boat prices.~The rocking image and sleepy sea support a calm response.
supported_humour~Read: 'Dad searched for his glasses. They were sitting on his nose.' Which reason fits finding this funny?~He is looking for something already on his face.~He is wearing a red coat.~He has broken his glasses.~The humour comes from the difference between what Dad thinks and where the glasses are.
improve_response~A reader says, 'The map was good.' Which addition makes the appreciation more useful?~It showed the route so I could follow the explorers.~It was somewhere inside the book.~Good maps are always good.~The added reason explains what the map helped the reader do.
opinion_fact~Which sentence expresses appreciation rather than only a fact about a book?~I enjoyed the lively dialogue between the sisters.~The book has two sisters in it.~The title contains four words.~Enjoyed expresses a personal response to a feature of the writing.
reason_link~Complete: 'I preferred the recipe with numbered steps because ___.'~I could follow the order easily~its page number was even~the other recipe also used flour~Numbered steps support an ordered sequence, giving a relevant reason for the preference.
compare_words~A reader felt a little uneasy, not terrified, during a storm scene. Which response matches?~The scene made me slightly nervous.~The scene frightened me more than anything ever has.~The scene made me completely relaxed.~Slightly nervous matches a mild response rather than extreme fear or calm.
author_choice~Read: 'The old door creaked. Something scratched on the other side.' Which appreciation connects to the author's choices?~I liked the suspense because the sounds made me wonder what was there.~I liked the clear answer because the creature was named.~I liked the recipe because each step was numbered.~Unexplained sounds create curiosity and tension.
ending_response~Read: 'After weeks of trying, the small bird finally flew.' Which response explains satisfaction with the ending?~I felt pleased because the bird's effort finally paid off.~I felt pleased because the story never ended.~I felt pleased because the bird gave up.~The ending resolves the bird's struggle with success.
support_preference~Which detail would help explain preferring a wildlife book for learning?~Clear captions explain what each animal is doing.~The cover happens to match my shoes.~My friend placed it on the top shelf.~Captions contribute to learning about the animals; the other details do not.
respectful_recommendation~Which recommendation gives another reader a useful reason?~Try this story if you enjoy solving clues before the detective.~Read it because everyone has to like my book.~Read it because books are made of paper.~The recommendation connects the story's feature to a possible reader interest.
text_specific_reason~Read: 'The train clattered, rattled and roared into the station.' Which comment appreciates these words?~The noisy verbs helped me hear the train in my mind.~The words explained the price of a ticket.~The words made the train seem silent.~The sound-rich verbs support an imagined sound experience.
@personal_response~Read: 'A paper boat sailed down the gutter. A beetle rode on top like a captain.' Say or write whether you enjoyed this tiny story and give a reason linked to its words.~Example: 'I enjoyed it because the beetle acting like a captain was funny.' A reasoned dislike is equally acceptable.~Accept an actual preference and a relevant reference to the boat, beetle, imagined role or language. Do not grade the child on agreeing with the model opinion.
@compare_response~Read A: 'The dog is wet.' Read B: 'Water dripped from the dog's whiskers onto my shoes.' Choose the version you prefer and explain why.~Example: 'I prefer B because I can picture where the water drips.' A may be preferred for its brevity with a reason.~Accept either choice with a relevant explanation of clarity, detail, effect or brevity.
test_poem~Read: 'Round and round the leaves all go, twirling high and drifting low.' Which response explains enjoyment?~I like the movement words because I can imagine the leaves dancing.~I like the instructions for planting a tree.~I like the facts about a leaf's roots.~The reader connects appreciation to the movement language actually present.
test_character~Read: 'Bo gave up his seat so the tired traveller could rest.' Which reason supports liking Bo?~He notices another person's need and helps.~He is the fastest runner in the story.~He tells the funniest joke.~The helpful action supports appreciation of Bo's thoughtfulness.
test_precision~A reader wants a word meaning 'full of exciting action'. Which response fits?~The rescue story was thrilling.~The rescue story was dull.~The rescue story was peaceful.~Thrilling expresses excitement; dull and peaceful do not match this intended response.
test_reason~Which response explains a preference for an audio version of a poem?~I could hear the rhythm clearly when it was read aloud.~The file was stored in a folder.~The poem existed before I listened.~The response relates the format to a feature the listener appreciates.
test_dislike~A reader dislikes an ending because the main problem is left unanswered. Which comment explains this?~I wanted to know whether the lost child got home, but the story stopped.~The book is wrong because I dislike it.~The writer should never write again.~The response identifies the unresolved question and its effect on the reader.
test_two_views~One reader likes a long description; another prefers the action. What can both readers do?~Give a text detail that explains their own preference.~Agree that only action belongs in stories.~Choose an opinion without discussing the text.~Different preferences can each be explained through text evidence.
test_evidence~Which line best supports 'I enjoyed the magical setting'?~Tiny stars grew on the branches like fruit.~The fence was painted last week.~A sign showed the park's opening times.~Stars growing on trees create an impossible, magical image.
test_improve~How could a reader improve 'I liked the ending'?~Add what happened at the end and why it felt satisfying.~Repeat 'I liked it' three more times.~List the page numbers without explaining them.~A specific event and its effect turn a bare preference into an explained response.
test_book_feature~A reader says, 'I liked being able to compare the two insects easily.' Which feature could support this?~Their facts appear side by side under matching headings.~Their names are hidden on separate unlabelled pages.~Only one insect is described.~Matching headings and adjacent information support comparison.
test_strength~Which comment expresses mild disappointment rather than complete dislike?~I liked most of it, but the ending was a little rushed.~I hated every part of it.~It was the best book I have ever read.~The first response limits the criticism to a small concern.
test_language_effect~Read: 'The kitten tucked one cold paw beneath its chin.' Which response connects feeling to a detail?~I felt sorry for the kitten because its paw was cold.~I felt excited because the kitten won a prize.~I felt amused because the kitten told a joke.~The cold paw supports sympathy; no prize or joke is mentioned.
test_comparison~A factual page explains volcanoes. A story imagines living inside one. Which preference gives a relevant reason?~I prefer the factual page because I want to learn how eruptions happen.~I prefer the story because the other text has letters.~I prefer the factual page because no one may enjoy stories.~The preference is connected to the reader's learning purpose.
test_recommendation~Who would most likely value 'I liked the repeated chorus because I could join in'?~Someone choosing a song to sing with a group.~Someone choosing a silent map to navigate.~Someone choosing a list of bus times.~A repeated chorus supports shared singing, so the reason helps that audience.
test_response_vs_retell~Which response includes appreciation rather than just retelling?~I enjoyed the clever trick when the mouse distracted the giant.~The mouse distracted the giant.~The giant looked away and the mouse ran.~Enjoyed and clever express evaluation of the event, beyond reporting it.
@test_preference~Read: 'The robot watered the garden with lemonade. By sunset, every flower was sticky.' Give your own response and a reason from the text.~Example: 'I found it funny because the robot used the wrong drink.' A supported dislike is also valid.~Accept a clear personal response linked to the robot's mistake, surprising result or language; do not require the model preference.
@test_reasoned_choice~Text A says, 'Owls hunt at night.' Text B says, 'Under the moon, an owl slipped silently between the trees.' Which would you prefer to read more of, and why?~Example: choose A for quick facts or B for its atmosphere and descriptive detail.~Accept either preference with a reason tied to a feature of the supplied text. Spelling is not the assessed target.
'''

HINTS={
 'LA01':'Notice the listener, the situation and the speaker\'s role.',
 'LA02':'Connect your response to a particular word, event or feature of the text.',
 'LA03':'Ask what the text helps its reader do and how its parts support that purpose.',
 'LA04':'Read the nearby words to work out who, what or which action is meant.',
 'LA05':'Match the information you need to the label, topic entry or page reference.'
}

def build():
 for suffix,source in sorted(DATA.items()):
  code='AC9E2'+suffix
  rows=[r.split('~') for r in source.strip().splitlines()]
  assert len(rows)==40,(code,len(rows))
  items=[]
  for i,row in enumerate(rows):
   bank='practice' if i<24 else 'test'
   n=i+1 if i<24 else i-23
   skill,question,*rest=row
   adult=skill.startswith('@')
   assert len(row)==(4 if adult else 6),(code,n,row)
   if adult:
    model,note=rest
    correct,wrong1,wrong2='Adult review required','Response not yet reviewed','Revise with an adult'
    summary=note
   else:correct,wrong1,wrong2,summary=rest
   # Rotations are balanced separately within each bank, including open tasks.
   position=(n-1)%3
   options=[wrong1,wrong2];options.insert(position,correct)
   item=dict(id=f'{code}-{bank[0].upper()}-{n:03}',subject='english',year_level='Year 2',curriculum_code=code,bank=bank,
    stage='apply' if adult else ('recognise' if n<=8 else 'connect'),skill=skill.lstrip('@'),
    question=question,audio_prompt=question,visual={'type':'none','alt_text':''},
    answers=[{'text':v,'is_correct':j==position} for j,v in enumerate(options)],correct_index=position,
    explanation={'summary':summary,'hint':HINTS[suffix]},difficulty=3 if adult else (1 if n<=8 else 2),
    difficulty_tier='transfer' if adult else ('confidence' if n<=8 else 'developing'),sequence_priority=n,
    quality_schema='year2-english-first5-original-v1')
   if adult:item.update(grading_mode='adult-review',model_answer=model,acceptance_note=note,
    response_instructions='Say your answer to an adult or type it here. An adult checks this task in Review answers.',completion_label='Ready for adult review')
   items.append(item)
  path=ROOT/'assets/assessment-banks/year2/english'/f'{code.lower()}.json'
  path.write_text(json.dumps(items,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2)+'\n')
  print(code,len(items),'questions;',sum('grading_mode' in q for q in items),'adult-reviewed tasks')

if __name__=='__main__':build()
